1
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:10,630
On the way to school, Lin passes two sets of traffic lights.

2
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:12,330
They work independently.

3
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:16,650
The chance each one is green when she arrives is 0.4.

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:19,610
What is the probability that both are green?

5
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:23,160
Pause the video and work it out.

6
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:26,520
0.4 times 0.4 is 0.16.

7
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:30,820
Remember: independent events do not affect each other.

8
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:35,770
For independent events, the probability of A and B is P of A times P of B.

9
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:40,800
A head and more than 2 on a dice: a half times 2 thirds is a third.

10
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:43,260
A tree diagram shows every outcome.

11
00:00:43,310 --> 00:00:46,070
Each light is green, G, or not green, N.

12
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:48,730
Not green has probability 0.6.

13
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:49,370
Your turn.

14
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:52,460
The first light is green, and the second is not.

15
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:53,560
What is P of G N?

16
00:00:53,610 --> 00:00:56,700
Pause the video and try it.

17
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:58,620
0.4 times 0.6 is 0.24.

18
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:01,060
Multiply along the branches.

19
00:01:01,110 --> 00:01:04,230
The four outcomes add up to 1.

20
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:07,130
To combine different routes, add.

21
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:08,470
Here is the method.

22
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:12,570
Step 1: draw a tree diagram with all the outcomes.

23
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:16,490
Step 2: branches from one point add up to 1.

24
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:19,570
Step 3: multiply along each route.

25
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:22,490
Step 4: add the routes you want.

26
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:25,990
A spinner has a probability of winning of a fifth.

27
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,180
It is spun twice.

28
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:30,880
The chance of winning both is 1 twenty-fifth.

29
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:36,720
The bus is late with probability 0.2, and it rains with probability 0.3.

30
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:38,420
They are independent.

31
00:01:38,470 --> 00:01:43,340
The chance of neither is 0.8 times 0.7, which is 0.56.

32
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:44,030
Your turn.

33
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,860
What is the probability that exactly one light is green?

34
00:01:47,910 --> 00:01:50,760
Pause and work it out.

35
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:53,180
Two routes: G N and N G.

36
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:56,050
0.24 plus 0.24 is 0.48.

37
00:01:56,100 --> 00:02:03,640
Over 50 school days, both lights are green on about 0.16 times 50, which is 8 days.

38
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:06,570
Each light is green with probability 0.4.

39
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:09,570
Emma finds the probability both are green.

40
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:11,540
Which line is wrong?

41
00:02:11,590 --> 00:02:12,690
Line 1 is wrong.

42
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:16,320
For and, with independent events, multiply.

43
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:19,680
0.4 times 0.4 is 0.16.

44
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:27,420
Engineers multiply the chances that independent parts fail, to work out how reliable a whole machine is.

45
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:28,660
One last question.

46
00:02:28,710 --> 00:02:32,580
What is the probability that at least one light is green?

47
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:35,850
Pause the video and answer.

48
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:39,360
At least one is everything except none.

49
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:41,810
1 minus 0.36 is 0.64.

50
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:46,280
To remember: for independent events, and means multiply.

51
00:02:46,330 --> 00:02:50,420
Tree diagrams: multiply along, add between routes.

52
00:02:50,470 --> 00:02:53,590
Branches from one point add up to 1.

53
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:56,510
At least one is 1 minus P of none.

54
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:01,990
Great work! Now practise lesson 12.3 on Camday.
